    "exercisePages": "105-108",
    "exerciseTitle": "Use it on the exercises.",
    "exerciseIntro": "There are five exercise types. Each asks for a **different skill**: recognition, spelling, tanween, reading or writing.",
    "exerciseTips": [
      {
        "title": "Recognize",
        "text": "Identify the character's shape and dots."
      },
      {
        "title": "Check its role",
        "text": "/ʔ/, long aa, or taaʔ marbuuṭah?"
      },
      {
        "title": "Apply the task",
        "text": "Circle, choose, add, read or spell."
      }
    ],
    "exerciseGuide": [
      {
        "exercise": 1,
        "pages": "105",
        "task": "Spot the special characters (circle the ones not part of the alphabet)",
        "method": "Circle the special forms, **not every regular Arabic letter**. Look for ء ؤ ئ ة ى and their connected shapes. The answer key circles six forms in the grid.",
        "example": {
          "ar": "ـئـ",
          "translit": "",
          "note": "CIRCLE · also ء ؤ ئ ة ـؤ (six in all)"
        },
        "contrast": {
          "ar": "أ",
          "translit": "",
          "note": "DO NOT CIRCLE · أ ـأ و ي ه are regular alphabet forms"
        }
      },
      {
        "exercise": 2,
        "pages": "105",
        "task": "Pick the correctly spelled word, then transcribe it",
        "method": "Check the mark on the letter **before the hamzah**: sukuun at the end -> ء, ḍammah -> ؤ, kasrah -> ئ. Then transcribe the correct option; the seat is read /ʔ/.",
        "example": {
          "ar": "فُؤَادْ",
          "translit": "fu ʔaad",
          "note": "#8 · فُ has ḍammah, so hamzah on waaw. The waaw seat gives **no** /w/ sound."
        },
        "contrast": {
          "ar": "مِلْء",
          "translit": "milʔ",
          "note": "#2 · ل has sukuun and hamzah is last -> free hamzah ء"
        }
      },
      {
        "exercise": 3,
        "pages": "106",
        "task": "Add tanween fatḥah, then transcribe",
        "method": "Add ـً, then decide on an extra ا. Regular ending -> add ا (#8 وَجْهًا, waj han). Ending in ة, ء or أ -> no ا by the basic rule. ئ / ؤ usually take ا. Key exceptions: شَيْئًا (#2), فَيْئًا (#3), وُضُوءًا (#4).",
        "example": {
          "ar": "مُعَلِّمَةً",
          "translit": "mu ʿal li ma tan",
          "note": "#1 · ends in ة -> no extra alif"
        },
        "contrast": {
          "ar": "شَيْئًا",
          "translit": "shay ʔan",
          "note": "#2 · EXCEPTION (p. 102): ء becomes ئ and takes alif"
        }
      },
      {
        "exercise": 4,
        "pages": "106-107",
        "task": "Transcribe what you see",
        "method": "Read every letter and vowel mark. The hamzah seat is /ʔ/, not an extra w or y. An undotted final ى sounds **aa**; a dotted ي after kasrah is **ii** (#6 أَمَامِي, ʔa maa mii).",
        "example": {
          "ar": "جَرَى",
          "translit": "ja raa",
          "note": "#4 · alif maqṣuurah = long aa"
        },
        "contrast": {
          "ar": "بُؤْسٌ",
          "translit": "buʔ sun",
          "note": "#3 · hamzah on waaw = /ʔ/, no w sound"
        }
      },
      {
        "exercise": 5,
        "pages": "107-108",
        "task": "Write the Arabic word from the transcription",
        "method": "Build the word syllable by syllable, then choose the **hamzah seat** from the vowel before it (ḍammah -> ؤ, kasrah -> ئ) and the final form. The transcription gives the sound; spelling also needs learned patterns.",
        "example": {
          "ar": "رُؤُوسٌ",
          "translit": "ru ʔuu sun",
          "note": "#2 · ḍammah before hamzah -> ؤ"
        },
        "contrast": {
          "ar": "مَدْرَسَةٌ",
          "translit": "mad ra sa tun",
          "note": "#14 · final taaʔ marbuuṭah"
        }
      }
    ],
    "exerciseFooter": "**What you can and cannot know:** You can **read** a printed special character by recognizing its shape. But you **cannot always derive its exact spelling from sound alone**. Chapter 4 gives useful beginner cues; it says there are exceptions and does not teach a complete hamzah-spelling system."
  },
